diagnosis_text:
A thorough four-phase broad search found no fault anywhere in this topology. Phase 1 (topology-wide reachability) confirmed 0% packet loss across all tested host pairs. Phase A infrastructure sweep found no ACL/firewall rules, no static ARP poisoning, no duplicate MACs, no addressing anomaly — both hosts have correct IPs, default routes, and point to the correct resolver (dns_server at 10.200.0.2). Phase B control-plane sweep found all six FRR routers healthy with ospfd running, all OSPF adjacencies in Full state, and 10 routes installed on each router; tc_snapshot found zero shaping or netem rules on any of the 30 inspected interfaces. Phase D service sweep confirmed that from host_1_1_1_1 (and host_2_1_1_1), all four names — web0.local, web1.local, web2.local, web3.local — resolve to the correct IPs in milliseconds, and all four HTTP requests return HTTP 200 with average total times of 3–6 ms. The DNS server is alive and responding correctly; pressure_sweep found no stress tools, no CPU spikes, and no socket floods on any device. The user's report of intermittent DNS sluggishness is not confirmed by any live evidence or residual mechanism trace. Root cause: there is no active fault — the system is fully healthy at the time of investigation, and no injected mechanism (tc delay, flap script, resource overload, misconfiguration) exists that would produce the claimed intermittency. Fix: no corrective action is warranted; do not restart the DNS server.
